<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hilary of Poitiers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 310–c. 367</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Hilarius Pictaviensis, Saint Hilary, Hammer of the Arians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hilary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> was a 4th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Poitiers and a Doctor of the Church, called the Hammer of the Arians for his opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>. Born at Poitiers to distinguished pagan parents, he turned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> through study of the Scriptures. Banished to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> after the synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a> in 356, he spent nearly four years in exile, writing De synodis and De trinitate, the first successful expression in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> of the Nicene theology worked out in Greek. Back in his diocese in 361, he opposed the homoion creed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and encouraged Martin, the future bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, to found a monastery at Ligugé; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> he died in Poitiers in 367.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hilary-of-poitiers.jpg" alt="Hilary of Poitiers" width="330" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard de Montbaston et collaborateurs. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hilaryofpoitiers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hilary was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, either at the end of the 3rd century or the beginning of the 4th. His parents were pagans of distinction, and he received a good education that included a high level of Greek. Through study of the Old and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> writings he abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a>, and he, his wife and his daughter, traditionally known as Abra of Poitiers, were baptised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> was becoming popular among Christians in this period, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a>, sponsored by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, had rejected it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 355 Hilary wrote to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> a remonstrance against the persecutions by which the Arians had sought to crush their opponents. At the synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a> in 356 an imperial rescript banished him, with Rhodanus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>. The traditional explanation is that he refused to subscribe to the condemnation of Athanasius and the Nicene faith. More recently several scholars have suggested that political opposition to Constantius and support for the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-silvanus">Claudius Silvanus</a> may have led to his exile.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> he continued to govern his diocese and attended several synods, including the council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> in 359, which saw the triumph of the homoion party. In 360 he failed to gain an audience with Constantius, and when the council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> ratified the decisions of Ariminum and Seleucia he answered with the bitter In Constantium, which attacked the emperor as Antichrist. His repeated requests for public debates with Ursacius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> proved so inconvenient that he was sent back to his diocese, which he reached about 361.</p><p>At home he spent two or three years persuading local clergy that the homoion confession was a cover for Arian subordinationism, and synods in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> condemned the creed of Ariminum. In 364 he accused Auxentius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, of heterodoxy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> summoned him to Milan, but Auxentius gave satisfactory answers. Hilary described the failure in a work published in 365.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His early Commentarius in Evangelium Matthaei, written before his exile, is the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> commentary on Matthew to survive entire. De synodis, written in 358 and early 359, reviews the creeds of the Eastern bishops and argues that the difference from orthodox belief was sometimes in words rather than ideas, which led him to counsel the western bishops to be more reserved in condemnation. De trinitate, in twelve books, composed in 359 and 360, was the first successful expression in Latin of the theology worked out in Greek. Some regard him as the first Latin Christian hymn writer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> recognised him as a Doctor of the Church in 1851, and his feast day is 13 January.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hilary of Poitiers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Latin translation is traditionally attributed to <b>Hilary of Poitiers</b> (c. 300–368), who may have learned the hymn while in the East in 359–360.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gloria-in-excelsis-deo">Gloria in excelsis Deo</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars of the 19th century proposed <b>Hilary of Poitiers</b> and Nicetas of Remesiana; manuscripts from the 10th century on link the hymn with the name Nicetas, a case defended in the 1890s by Germain Morin, but hymnologists of the 20th century, especially Ernst Kähler (1958), showed that link…</blockquote><a href="/songs/te-deum">Te Deum</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=347" aria-label="Poitiers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4558 526 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hilary of Poitiers"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5008 796h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5008 796h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.58,0.34&amp;year=347">What was here in 347 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
